{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:04:22.022567Z","id":"b4a15cbd6ff7f606479d06ce62805cf91f55b141a8de22b9fa3d9d17d2b9a6ca","body":{"detail":"serde_json serializes struct fields in declaration order. AnnotationBody fields are declared alphabetically (detail, kind, ref, references, span, suggested_fix, summary, supersedes, tags), and that ordering IS the Metabox canonical body form per SPEC §canonical-form. The doc comment on line 244 says 'Field order is alphabetical (MCF canonical form)' but nothing prevents a future contributor from grouping fields semantically (e.g., putting 'kind' first). Such a reorder would silently change every newly-minted record's id and invalidate every existing record's id. EpochBody and DependencyBody have the same fragility.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":243},"end":{"line":262},"content_hash":"8e674cc061528ba83df5271a15242c8d42009b368805a8a09e6907a599f982c9"},"suggested_fix":"Add a unit test that pins the canonical id for a fixed AnnotationBody/EpochBody/DependencyBody triple (with all fields populated) — analogous to the dependency golden id in tests/integration.rs but covering each body type. Any field reorder will then break the test loudly. Alternative: serialize bodies via a BTreeMap or a hand-written Serialize impl that doesn't rely on declaration order, decoupling the id from struct layout.","summary":"AnnotationBody field declaration order silently determines MCF canonical IDs — make the invariant load-bearing in code or in a doc-test","tags":["review","invariant","canonical-form"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/qual_file.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:04:52.981430Z","id":"51596621d397cf8d6f0ec960a74945b637a03a419daae0212de4fc6d94ed8ccb","body":{"detail":"subject_name() uses to_string_lossy() and then checks 'stripped.ends_with('/') || stripped.ends_with(MAIN_SEPARATOR)'. On Windows, MAIN_SEPARATOR is '\\\\', so a path like 'src\\\\.qual' yields a subject of 'src\\\\' rather than 'src/'. Tests at qual_file.rs:332 and the recommended directory layout in agents/concepts.md ('src/.qual -> src/') treat the trailing '/' as canonical. A subject keyed by 'src\\\\' would not match any annotation lookup keyed by 'src/' (and vice versa). This is a portability defect, since other lookups (find_annotations_for, by_subject in ls.rs) compare subjects by exact string equality.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":228},"end":{"line":245},"content_hash":"6d25802318273ee4d04ce1b55087ac6b3ebd0d2a1dbd0240ebaf82a8e2500691"},"suggested_fix":"Normalize the separator before comparing/forming the subject — replace backslashes with forward slashes (via .replace('\\\\', \"/\") on the lossy string, or by walking Path components and rejoining with '/'). Apply consistently in subject_name(), resolve_qual_path(), and find_qual_file_for(). Add a Windows-targeted test that exercises 'src\\\\.qual' directly.","summary":"subject_name() yields backslash-suffixed subjects on Windows, breaking the trailing-'/' convention","tags":["review","portability"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428047Z","id":"03f495adbb90e75950573cf37150dcb05b1fb5c5f89e7e078f2d8cafd590d7a7","body":{"detail":"Failure: record a concern C, `qualifier reply <C>` with a comment R (R.references = C), then `qualifier resolve <C>` (resolve supersedes C). `qualifier threads x.rs` shows 1 thread (0 open). Run `qualifier compact x.rs`: prune() keeps only records filter_superseded() returns, so C is deleted while R and the resolve survive. `qualifier threads x.rs` now prints `[676d2a3c] comment x.rs a reply comment` and `1 thread (1 open)`. Cause: threads.rs is_reply()/parent_of() only treat `references`/`supersedes` as links when the target ID exists (src/threads.rs:183-193), so R becomes its own origin and, being a non-resolve tip, an open thread; the resolve becomes a separate closed thread whose root is itself. Reproduced against target/debug/qualifier 0.8.0. Closed review findings resurface as open work after every compaction, and the original concern's summary/span is lost from the thread view.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":32},"end":{"line":53},"content_hash":"bed8c84ff8ffa804092473370355c62bfa6fb927f5655fe964d4fa9c5eb1b0c1"},"suggested_fix":"In prune(), keep superseded records that are still the `references` target of a surviving record (or the target of a surviving resolve), or rewrite those replies' parent as the chain tip before dropping it. Alternatively, have prune drop whole closed threads (root chain + replies) together. Add a regression test in tests/cli_integration.rs: record, reply, resolve, compact, assert `threads` reports 0 open.","summary":"prune drops the root of a resolved thread, so its replies reappear as a new open thread after `qualifier compact`","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/content_hash.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428141Z","id":"d4c70f613795b761bc541c36fe169ddbf5261899c2e5a8c37b624d7931f0e92b","body":{"detail":"Failure: in a repo with a 6-line x.rs, `qualifier record concern x.rs:5:2 \"reversed\"` exits 101 with `panicked at src/content_hash.rs:21:40: slice index starts at 4 but ends at 2`. compute_span_hash checks start==0 and both bounds against EOF but never start <= end, so `all_lines[start - 1..=end - 1]` panics whenever start > end + 1. validate() (src/annotation.rs:675-689) does not reject end.line < start.line either (nor end.col == 0), and record.rs computes the hash before validating, so no error message ever reaches the user. A hand-written or third-party record with a reversed span would also panic `qualifier freshness`/`diff` via check_freshness.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":14},"end":{"line":21},"content_hash":"fad96345f0e7cafd2337762933c967c87993580aa129909ee7da193800449e2e"},"suggested_fix":"Return None from compute_span_hash when end < start (and have check_freshness report a Missing reason like `span end precedes start`). Add `span.end.line must be >= span.start.line` (and end.col >= 1, plus start.col <= end.col on a single line) to validate(), and reject reversed spans in parse_span/parse_location callers before hashing.","summary":"compute_span_hash panics when span end line is before start line; `qualifier record` crashes on a reversed span","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428242Z","id":"b913d1d1782800e4de9bbc9f86d08f09728ec08674a605de4bee413c4b106a42","body":{"detail":"Failure: a .qual file containing `{\"metabox\":\"1\",\"type\":\"annotation\",...,\"body\":{\"kind\":\"concern\",\"score\":-20,\"summary\":\"custom field\"}}` plus any superseded record; running `qualifier compact x.rs` rewrites the line as `\"body\":{\"kind\":\"concern\",\"summary\":\"custom field\"}` -- `score` is gone, and the stored `id` no longer matches the rewritten content. AnnotationBody (and the Annotation envelope) has no catch-all field, so serde drops unknown keys on deserialize and write_all() (src/qual_file.rs:74-81) re-serializes the lossy struct. SPEC.md section 4 says tools layer quality signals 'by adding custom body fields', and SPEC 2.5 says data implementations do not understand MUST be preserved during compaction/rewriting. generate_id() also hashes the lossy struct, so qualifier computes a different ID than any MCF-conforming tool for such records.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":243},"end":{"line":262},"content_hash":"8e674cc061528ba83df5271a15242c8d42009b368805a8a09e6907a599f982c9"},"suggested_fix":"Add `#[serde(flatten)] pub extra: std::collections::BTreeMap<String, serde_json::Value>` to AnnotationBody (and EpochBody/DependencyBody, plus an envelope-level extra map if envelope extensions are allowed), and serialize so the canonical view still emits all body keys in lexicographic order (e.g., build the body as a BTreeMap<String, Value> in the canonical view). Update SPEC section 7 and the make_att()/make_record() helpers; add a round-trip test that compacts a record with a custom body field and asserts it and its ID survive.","summary":"Custom annotation body fields are silently discarded on any rewrite, contrary to SPEC section 4 and the forward-compatibility rule","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428316Z","id":"123d0309c1262da2f7f1b317f7dbcd821da3f765e0bb1e22198c6b50951a0333","body":{"detail":"created_at is parsed into DateTime<Utc> and re-emitted by chrono, which normalizes offsets to `Z` and pads fractional seconds (a file value `2026-02-24T10:00:00.5+00:00` is rewritten by `qualifier compact` as `2026-02-24T10:00:00.500Z`, verified with target/debug/qualifier 0.8.0). SPEC.md 2.8.1 rules 1-8 cover field order, whitespace, strings and integers but say nothing about timestamps, and SPEC table row for created_at only says 'RFC 3339 timestamp'. A non-Rust implementation that hashes the timestamp bytes as written (the natural reading of MCF) will compute a different ID than generate_id() for any record not written by chrono, and qualifier silently changes the bytes on rewrite.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":546},"end":{"line":559},"content_hash":"1d119c7e432f959848edc2d0ca8ae0613f6da8e75ce3c145fcaa0c3d97ab5c94"},"suggested_fix":"Add an MCF rule to SPEC.md 2.8.1 (and METABOX.md) fixing the timestamp form, e.g. UTC with `Z`, and either no fractional part or exactly the fractional digits chrono's AutoSi emits; bump the spec version. Have validate() or parse reject or flag non-canonical timestamps rather than silently re-encoding them, and add a golden-ID test with fractional seconds.","summary":"MCF hashes created_at as chrono re-serializes it, but SPEC 2.8.1 never defines a canonical timestamp form, so IDs are implementation-defined","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/qual_file.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428368Z","id":"cbacf8e797b8a1640b7d1c346f7a16a4e6d270d58af94bdfbe9531aaf6b1b442","body":{"detail":"discover() calls parse() with `?` for each file, and parse() aborts on the first line serde rejects (src/qual_file.rs:32-34). Reproduced: adding other/.qual with an annotation missing `summary` makes `qualifier threads x.rs` (an unrelated subject) exit 1 with `other/.qual:1: missing field summary`. The same happens with leftover merge-conflict markers in a repo without the union-merge config SPEC.md 8.2 recommends, or with a record from a newer tool that adds a required-looking field. SPEC 2.5 asks implementations to tolerate records they do not understand; here one bad record blocks all annotations everywhere.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":210},"end":{"line":220},"content_hash":"80ab040eeb59e6dc42f6f6eab77e9f779efa3ea7da86720c80c2160678fcc125"},"suggested_fix":"Make parse() collect per-line errors (e.g., return QualFile plus Vec<ParseError> or keep bad lines as Record::Unknown/raw passthrough), have discover() warn and continue, and keep strict failure only for write paths that would rewrite the broken file (compact). Add a CLI test that a malformed sibling .qual produces a warning, not a failure.","summary":"One malformed line in any .qual file makes discover() fail, so every read command errors for the whole repository","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428408Z","id":"87c800985a350ca1b3fdeea00a29399ea73058079113dc0708d4f66494e1c1fe","body":{"detail":"SPEC.md 3.3 says compaction prunes superseded records and then, with --snapshot, replaces 'all surviving records for each subject' with an epoch. snapshot() groups every Annotation/Epoch record without calling filter_superseded(), so epoch.body.refs lists superseded IDs and the summary `Compacted from N records` counts them (test_snapshot_with_supersession_chain at src/compact.rs:256 shows a 3-link chain counted as 3). The epoch's refs then do not describe the net state the epoch claims to summarize.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":55},"end":{"line":104},"content_hash":"a90bf0691b582559d6551e07cc6e94c2a5845e2ef23f524953681f5a57647fe3"},"suggested_fix":"Run filter_superseded() over the file's records first and build each subject's epoch from the active annotation/epoch records only; update test_snapshot_with_supersession_chain to expect refs = [tip]. If including superseded IDs is intended, change SPEC 3.3 wording instead.","summary":"snapshot() folds superseded records into the epoch instead of pruning first as SPEC 3.3 describes","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/content_hash.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428441Z","id":"2eb548399f05136c9bec3a16cefa7ee3d49c792cf36b4c7695d9208b6faef1d2","body":{"detail":"compute_span_hash returns None for unreadable files, non-UTF-8 content (read_to_string fails), start == 0, and spans past EOF alike. check_freshness then only distinguishes 'file not found' and labels every other case `span lines X..=Y beyond file length`, so a Latin-1 source file or a permission error is reported to `qualifier freshness` users as a line-range problem.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":54},"end":{"line":68},"content_hash":"4252e3a64872468afcf9b1dc0600fcf0eb8a24904db7edb918b692e96dc81506"},"suggested_fix":"Have compute_span_hash return a Result with a small error enum (Io, NotUtf8, OutOfRange, Reversed) and map each to a distinct Missing reason; or read bytes with fs::read and split on b'\\n' so non-UTF-8 files hash instead of failing.","summary":"check_freshness reports 'beyond file length' for any read failure on an existing file","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428501Z","id":"ef89cae45e48a2aff34cc52f22ba80d0a87f74c333b916dc9c8004446e7be3ef","body":{"detail":"The `known` list in validate() contains pass, fail, blocker, concern, comment, praise, suggestion, waiver but not resolve. `qualifier record resovle ...` (edit distance 2) is accepted as Kind::Custom(\"resovle\"); threads.rs treats only Kind::Resolve as closing (src/threads.rs:125), so the supersede leaves the thread open with the typo record as its root.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":647},"end":{"line":663},"content_hash":"786f7032b486e7843c6e03580ba05a339ac4b02078c3b0f7db4f558f78026390"},"suggested_fix":"Add \"resolve\" to the known list, or derive the list from the Kind variants so a new built-in kind cannot be missed.","summary":"Kind typo detection omits `resolve`, so a misspelled resolve silently becomes a custom kind and never closes the thread","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428550Z","id":"546e4f8f43619b85fcdabc0337d95e1b4ac1221828a260da22d3bd9242cedf50","body":{"detail":"For each record with `supersedes`, the walk calls `records.iter().find(|r| r.id() == current)` per hop (line 752), giving O(N * L * N) for N records and chain length L. src/cli/targets.rs:401-405 runs it against the entire target .qual file on every reply/resolve/supersede, and directory-level .qual files accumulate every record for a directory. Also, since each start re-walks shared chain suffixes, cycle-free long chains cost O(L^2).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":724},"end":{"line":764},"content_hash":"b56638a096da3297565f5c4091f0d50c10e1e726d2bf3eef55150e5e453564cb"},"suggested_fix":"Build a HashMap<&str, &str> id -> supersedes once and do a single colored DFS (or walk with a global `done` set so each node is visited once).","summary":"check_supersession_cycles rescans the whole record list per chain hop, making every supersedes write quadratic in file size","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/qual_file.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:11.428602Z","id":"5ae55ef5fbd1e03c9b39030a32f25ca0b35ac1b467c1d167151bbe7789322a7b","body":{"detail":"AGENTS.md requires SPEC section 7 to match public signatures. Not listed there: qual_file::{parse_str, write_all, resolve_qual_path, find_records_for, find_annotations_for, find_qual_file_for, subject_name, find_project_root, detect_vcs}; annotation::{parse_span, parse_location, check_supersession_cycles, validate_supersession_targets, Span::end_or_start, Span::normalize, Record::record_type}; and crate::Error / crate::Result (src/lib.rs:10-27). Library users reading SPEC cannot discover the write or validation entry points.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":290},"content_hash":"97086bccc3f6725c34f3807c4146ae904beb2b615e1e1f0f7b036878238ef8e8"},"suggested_fix":"Add the missing signatures to SPEC.md section 7, or narrow visibility (pub(crate)) for helpers that are CLI-only, such as resolve_qual_path and find_qual_file_for.","summary":"SPEC.md section 7 (Library API) omits many public functions the crate exports","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480474Z","id":"4fd9a32852a6246c65df4f89ff13f2ce85ceeb47066d3bf0bf2cc9c6d311707a","body":{"detail":"Reproduced with target/debug/qualifier 0.8.0 in a fresh git repo: `record concern x.rs:2`, `reply <C> \"a reply comment\"`, `resolve <C>` -> `qualifier threads x.rs` prints `0 threads (0 open)`. `qualifier compact x.rs` prints `3 -> 2 records (1 superseded, pruned)`; `qualifier threads x.rs` then prints `[54736303] comment x.rs a reply comment` / `1 thread (1 open)`. Cause: src/compact.rs:36-53 prune() keeps only filter_superseded() output, and src/threads.rs:183-193 is_reply()/parent_of() only link to IDs present in the file, so the orphaned reply becomes its own open thread.","kind":"comment","references":"03f495adbb90e75950573cf37150dcb05b1fb5c5f89e7e078f2d8cafd590d7a7","summary":"confirmed: compact prunes a resolved thread's root and its reply resurfaces as an open thread","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/content_hash.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480506Z","id":"0e99879c0d0cd82e3be2bf0550b5fa699fba8b2a9a439d5897bc4a8558d131ae","body":{"detail":"Reproduced: 6-line x.rs, `qualifier record concern x.rs:5:2 reversed` -> `panicked at src/content_hash.rs:21:40: slice index starts at 4 but ends at 2`, exit 101. src/content_hash.rs:17 checks start==0 and both bounds against EOF but not start<=end; src/cli/commands/record.rs:156 hashes before validate() at line 204, and validate() (src/annotation.rs:675-689) has no end>=start check.","kind":"comment","references":"d4c70f613795b761bc541c36fe169ddbf5261899c2e5a8c37b624d7931f0e92b","summary":"confirmed: qualifier record panics on a reversed span","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480517Z","id":"da21e94385a575e69f2f607ce176ea087a0e4b2557195fc4dd02f71ed9c55fc6","body":{"detail":"Reproduced: x.rs.qual with a record whose body is {\"kind\":\"concern\",\"score\":-20,\"summary\":\"custom field\"} plus a superseded pair; `qualifier compact x.rs` rewrote it as `\"body\":{\"kind\":\"concern\",\"summary\":\"custom field\"}` -- `score` is gone. AnnotationBody (src/annotation.rs:243-262) has no flatten/extra map, and write_all (src/qual_file.rs:74-81) re-serializes the struct. SPEC.md section 4 (line ~756) describes tools layering signals 'by adding custom body fields', so this is user data loss on a documented use.","kind":"comment","references":"b913d1d1782800e4de9bbc9f86d08f09728ec08674a605de4bee413c4b106a42","summary":"confirmed: compact drops custom annotation body fields","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480530Z","id":"b7ea64347f2074860ccbef7ae875af50d5b596cc54dd42bf6a935971d765ac55","body":{"detail":"Reproduced: a record with created_at `2026-02-24T10:00:00.5+00:00` was rewritten by `qualifier compact` as `2026-02-24T10:00:00.500Z`. generate_id (src/annotation.rs:546-559) serializes the DateTime<Utc>, not the source bytes. SPEC.md 2.8.1 (lines 399-437) rules 1-8 contain no timestamp rule.","kind":"comment","references":"123d0309c1262da2f7f1b317f7dbcd821da3f765e0bb1e22198c6b50951a0333","summary":"confirmed: timestamps are re-encoded by chrono and MCF does not define their canonical form","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/qual_file.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480538Z","id":"44256accbb125f2c42daf57f8e8fd8e56803834fd9e27522b64f08a16482bdc8","body":{"detail":"Reproduced: added other/.qual with an annotation lacking `summary`; `qualifier threads x.rs` (unrelated subject) exits 1 with `other/.qual:1: missing field summary`. parse() returns on the first bad line (src/qual_file.rs:32-34) and discover() propagates with `?` (src/qual_file.rs:218).","kind":"comment","references":"cbacf8e797b8a1640b7d1c346f7a16a4e6d270d58af94bdfbe9531aaf6b1b442","summary":"confirmed: one malformed .qual line makes every discover-based command fail","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480547Z","id":"434a23402d1c19f511526448bf53e33cd43fce3bc314d651cb404c71eef3e159","body":{"detail":"src/compact.rs:76-82 groups every Annotation/Epoch without filter_superseded(); src/cli/commands/compact.rs:70-71 calls snapshot() directly, not after prune(). test_snapshot_with_supersession_chain (src/compact.rs:256-265) asserts refs.len()==3 for a 3-link chain. SPEC.md 3.3 (lines 612-622) says prune first, then snapshot 'all surviving records'.","kind":"comment","references":"87c800985a350ca1b3fdeea00a29399ea73058079113dc0708d4f66494e1c1fe","summary":"confirmed: snapshot includes superseded records in the epoch refs and count","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/content_hash.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480559Z","id":"c5504e7e5f83df1a8ba637f22bbcf2d85276bf0d3d89dc728c7cbdf5740a8e14","body":{"detail":"Reproduced: recorded concern on y.rs:2 of a 3-line file, rewrote line 2 as byte 0xE9; `qualifier review --format json` reports `\"reason\": \"span lines 2..=2 beyond file length\"`. src/content_hash.rs:11 read_to_string(...).ok()? collapses read errors into None, and lines 55-66 label every non-missing-file None as out of range. (The command is `qualifier review`; there is no `freshness` subcommand.)","kind":"comment","references":"2eb548399f05136c9bec3a16cefa7ee3d49c792cf36b4c7695d9208b6faef1d2","summary":"confirmed: non-UTF-8 file is reported as span beyond file length","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480567Z","id":"357cb527338a6eb6e1205407aefc589bb21a6bad343a181d02d225cca2c6ee9f","body":{"detail":"Reproduced: `qualifier record resovle x.rs:3 typo` exits 0 and writes kind `resovle`. The known list at src/annotation.rs:648-657 omits `resolve`, while `pass`/`concern` etc. get the did-you-mean error.","kind":"comment","references":"ef89cae45e48a2aff34cc52f22ba80d0a87f74c333b916dc9c8004446e7be3ef","summary":"confirmed: `resovle` is accepted silently as a custom kind","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/annotation.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480576Z","id":"3aac7c70600d551af4c65a85ef25f85dbb42c812697736674337a8face4aec60","body":{"detail":"src/annotation.rs:752 `records.iter().find(|r| r.id() == current)` inside the per-record chain walk (lines 729-761), with no shared visited set across starts. src/cli/targets.rs:401-405 check_supersession runs it over the whole target file plus the new record.","kind":"comment","references":"546e4f8f43619b85fcdabc0337d95e1b4ac1221828a260da22d3bd9242cedf50","summary":"confirmed: cycle check does a linear scan per chain hop","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/qual_file.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:03.480584Z","id":"3888da42a788a770f44b7c1b5608d917275b514ae623efbddb66bdb723153683","body":{"detail":"SPEC.md section 7 (lines 1270-1420) lists for qual_file only QualFile, parse, append, discover, and no Error/Result. Yet src/qual_file.rs exports pub parse_str (46), write_all (74), resolve_qual_path (91), find_records_for (129), find_annotations_for (140), find_qual_file_for (153), subject_name (229), find_project_root (253), detect_vcs (274); src/annotation.rs exports parse_span (48), parse_location (106), check_supersession_cycles (726), validate_supersession_targets (769), Span::end_or_start (35), Span::normalize (40), Record::record_type (487); src/lib.rs:11-27 exports Error and Result.","kind":"comment","references":"5ae55ef5fbd1e03c9b39030a32f25ca0b35ac1b467c1d167151bbe7789322a7b","summary":"confirmed: SPEC section 7 omits many public library functions","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
